WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.000
What we received in The Rise of Skywalker
a redemption arc for Kylo Ren and a romantic

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.820
connection between him and Rey was a fairly
logical path to take, despite its divisive

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:10.320
nature.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:15.200
A long, long time ago in an alleged script
far away, however, their dynamic was very

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:15.700
different.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:21.199
YouTube user and former screenplay reader
Rob Burnett posted a video in which he claims

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.140
to have acquired a defunct draft of The Rise
of Skywalker's script.

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:28.520
The script, called Duel of the Fates, was
reportedly written back when Colin Trevorrow

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:31.080
was attached to the film as director in 2016.

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:35.280
Of course, there's no possible way to know
how genuine the alleged script is, unless

00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:38.970
the director himself or Disney officially
releases a statement.

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:41.500
The thing his, the director himself kinda
did.

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:45.880
Trevorrow confirmed on Twitter that leaked
concept art is indeed from “Duel Of The

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:48.620
Fates” - even using that alleged movie title.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:52.880
But the script leak happened before the concept
art did, and so did that title.

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:56.400
So did Trevorrow just sort of “low key”
confirm the script leak?

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:57.100
Kinda?

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:57.820
Sorta?

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:01.460
Well, let’s just say that you can believe
what you’d like about the following Reylo

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:02.200
info.

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:07.100
The biggest single difference in characterization
choices for this alleged early draft is the

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:09.200
fact that Kylo Ren isn't redeemed.

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:13.820
At one point while assessing the crushed Darth
Vader mask, Kylo acknowledges that what changed

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:18.390
Vader at the end of his life was love and
that was a weakness and failing on his part.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:22.780
He destroys the mask and vows that he will
succeed where his grandfather failed in achieving

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:26.060
power and becoming the strongest Force-user
in the galaxy.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:30.540
That doesn't mean that people don't make a
similar effort to save Kylo Ren. Rey spends

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:33.080
most of the film believing Kylo Ren can be
saved.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:37.770
The alleged script seems to make a big deal
of separating Ben Solo from Kylo Ren, to the

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:40.500
point that it might be a literal separate
personality.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:45.020
Additionally, Luke Skywalker has a significant
part to play in this theoretical story as

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:46.900
a Force ghost haunting Kylo Ren.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:51.660
Luke makes a lot of efforts to sway Kylo himself,
sometimes even reaching out to Rey through

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:55.820
the Force to make them connect and convince
Kylo to walk away from the dark side.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:57.580
Unfortunately, none of it works.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:02.680
Apparently, Duel of the Fates was interested
in making Rey's journey even more difficult

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:05.130
than the one she went through in The Rise
of Skywalker.

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:09.280
Rey has recurring, undescribed nightmares
in this alleged script and though Burnett

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:13.500
doesn't go into a lot of detail, these dreams
are meant to lead Rey to the mysterious realm

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:14.000
Mortis.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.200
The ethereal realm was last seen in The Clone
Wars animated series, where it's implied to

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:19.880
be the birthplace of the Force.

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:23.520
A crisis of confidence is at the heart of
Rey's character struggle as the weight of

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:25.620
her responsibility bears down upon her.

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:29.880
She has a conversation with Luke's Force ghost
while training in which she argues that the

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.860
demands of the Jedi path ask too much of any
one individual.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:37.780
Luke says her destiny is to be the last Jedi
and attain greatness; Rey replies that maybe

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:41.060
the last Jedi has already been lost, referring
to Luke's death.

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:45.440
Overall, this theoretical story puts a lot
more effort into presenting Rey as a Grey

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:50.410
Jedi, similar to Ahsoka Tano, who's unsatisfied
with the paradigm of the traditional Jedi

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:51.020
mantra.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:56.140
Another deviation in this alleged script is
that Kylo Ren and Rey never seem to have any

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:58.800
romantic interest, one-sided or mutual.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:02.989
Though Rey does seem to express sympathy for
Kylo Ren and wants to bring him back from

00:03:02.989 --> 00:03:07.370
the Dark Side, that interest seems to come
from the experiences she had in The Last Jedi.

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:11.630
That said, she does kill Kylo Ren at the end
of Duel of the Fates, though Burnett doesn't

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:14.600
specify in his summary how Rey feels about
the action.

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:19.600
One of the most frustrating aspects of The
Rise of Skywalker for critics was seeing director

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:24.260
J.J. Abrams seemingly throw away many of the
themes and plots of The Last Jedi.

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:29.620
When J.J. Abrams returned to the director's
chair after Trevorrow was let go, any previously

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:32.580
existing script was discarded and re-started
from scratch.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:37.200
However, Duel of the Fates apparently maintained
at least some of the character themes presented

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:38.020
in The Last Jedi.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:40.660
For one, there's no Force dyad in this script
at all.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:45.640
Kylo Ren and Rey are indeed the two most powerful
Force users of their generation, but this

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:48.209
isn't borne of a genetic destiny or prophecy.

00:03:48.209 --> 00:03:51.800
They both will have to work for it, which
is what the trip to Mortis is for.

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:55.490
The ultimate expression of their powers is
learned there: the ability to give and take

00:03:55.490 --> 00:03:58.640
pure life energy through the Force from other
living creatures.

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:03.140
This ability is the main tension of their
final lightsaber fight in which Kylo Ren dies.

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:07.400
The two of them are working toward this apex
of ability for themselves, not in pursuit

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:11.709
of titles or even necessarily for the political
systems they each represent.

00:04:11.709 --> 00:04:16.709
Kylo supposedly mocks General Hux, for his
aspirations to rule the galaxy and goes AWOL

00:04:16.709 --> 00:04:19.199
on the First Order in pursuit of his own interests.

00:04:19.199 --> 00:04:22.800
Rey simply needs to understand if she is everything
everyone believes she is.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:27.479
The last point of interest is that Duel of
the Fates remains true to The Last Jedi's

00:04:27.479 --> 00:04:30.180
revelation that Rey has no important lineage.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:34.229
The only fact we ever learn about her parents
is that Kylo confesses to murdering them at

00:04:34.229 --> 00:04:35.229
Snoke's behest.

00:04:35.229 --> 00:04:39.580
Rey even reiterates dialogue she had in The
Last Jedi that she is nobody, from nowhere.

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:40.760
"Where are you from?"

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:41.900
"Nowhere."

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:43.760
"No one's from nowhere."

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:44.460
"Jakku."

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:46.840
"Okay, that is pretty much nowhere."

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:51.340
That fact is part of what drives her crisis
of faith in the film: how can she possibly

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:54.320
be this important person if she's really no
one?

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:57.380
"They're dead in a pauper's desert in Jakku.

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:00.280
You have no place in this story.

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:01.160
You come from nothing.

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:02.160
You're nothing.”

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:06.320
Regardless of this alleged script's veracity,
we'll never see another version of The Rise

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:07.330
of Skywalker.

00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:10.869
Still, that doesn't mean we can't entertain
ourselves with different ideas about what

00:05:10.869 --> 00:05:14.740
happened a long, long time ago in a galaxy
far, far away.

